So if `lines_read` is `"full,10-20"`, then `normalized` becomes `"full,10-20"`, and `normalized.split(",")` gives `["full", "10-20"]`, so `"full" in ["full", "10-20"]` is `True`, so it returns `False` (don't read).

But wait! What if `lines_read` is `"10-20,full"`? Then `normalized` is `"10-20,full"`, split gives `["10-20", "full"]`, `"full" in ["10-20", "full"]` is `True`, so same result.

So the bug is: **The code accepts malformed input like `"full,10-20"` and treats it as "file read fully"**. This could lead to incorrect behavior if someone accidentally writes `"full,10-20"` instead of just `"full"` or `"10-20"`.

